  "articleBody": "Installing natural stone tile can really elevate a space, but there are some common pitfalls you'll want to avoid to make sure your project looks amazing and lasts. One of the biggest mistakes we see is skipping proper substrate preparation. Your subfloor absolutely has to be flat, clean, and structurally sound. Ignoring this can lead to cracks, uneven tiles, or even structural issues down the line, especially with the weight of natural stone. Another biggie is using the wrong adhesive or not accounting for movement. Natural stone can be heavy and sometimes requires specialized thin-sets, and you need to plan for movement joints, particularly in large areas or outdoors, to prevent cracking from expansion and contraction. Don't try to overlay new stone tile over loose existing tiles either; that's just asking for bond failure. Also, many folks forget about moisture management, especially in wet areas like bathrooms. Proper waterproofing is crucial before laying stone in these spots. And finally, not understanding the sealing and maintenance requirements for your specific stone type is a common error. Natural stone usually needs to be sealed to protect it. If you're unsure about structural assessments, waterproofing, or sealing strategies, it's really smart to bring in a professional. HH Remodeling LLC can guide you through the process and help you avoid these costly mistakes.",
